\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 1  Abstract\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 Utilization of glycerol, as the primary by-product of biodiesel, plays an important role in valorizing bioenergy manufacture. In our prior work, conversion glycerol to hydrocarbon (GTH) fuels was performed over bifunctional catalysts (Pt/H-ZSM-5 and Pd/H-ZSM-5). The Pd/H-ZSM-5 catalysts showed an optimal 60% yield of hydrocarbon fuels. In the present work, using Pd/H-ZSM-5 catalyst, GTH reaction network and lumped kinetic model are developed based on differential operating conditions. Rate constants, reaction orders and activation energies are obtained for each individual reaction step. Predicted values match well with experimental data well. Reaction mechanism proposed by the present work is discussed and compared with literature. The present kinetics work provides a practical approach for converting glycerol to hydrocarbon fuels.\par
\column
\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb300 \fi0 2  Introduction\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 With the growing demand for renewable sources of energy in recent years, bio-fuels have have emerged as promising alternative sources of fossil fuels. In addition to energy area, biodiesel, a typical bio-fuel, plays an important role in reducing the U.S. and global CO{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} footprint 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xue2011 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{1}{2011}{{Xue et\~al.}}{{Xue, Grift, and Hansen}}}}}
]. As reported recently, biodiesel from soy oil can result in 57% reduction in greenhouse gases as compared to petroleum diesel, while biodiesel produced from waste grease can result in an 86% reduction. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_EPA2010 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{2}{2010}{{EPA}}{{}}}}}
] Other benefits from biodiesel, such as the increases of energy security, renewable, biodegradable and non-toxic properties, additionally make it more attractive 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Al_Mulali2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{3}{2015}{{Al-Mulali}}{{}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Kralova2010 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{4}{2010}{{Kralova and Sjoblom}}{{Kralova, and Sjoblom}}}}}
]. As a result of biodiesel manufacture, about 10 wt% crude glycerol (approximately 0.75 lb glycerol per gallon) of biodiesel, is generated as an undesired byproduct. The enormous increase in annual global production of biodiesel during the last decade has resulted in a large surplus and low price of crude glycerol, which was about 1 cent per lb at the end of 2014 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Ardi2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{5}{2015}{{Ardi et\~al.}}{{Ardi, Aroua, and Hashim}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Kong2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{6}{2016}{{Kong et\~al.}}{{Kong, Aroua, and Daud}}}}}
].\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 To enhance biodiesel industry economics, therefore, it is important to valorize not only biodiesel product itself, but also to utilize the by-product crude glycerol 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Thanh2012 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{7}{2012}{{Thanh et\~al.}}{{Thanh, Okitsu, Boi, and Maeda}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Garlapati2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{8}{2016}{{Garlapati et\~al.}}{{Garlapati, Shankar, and Budhiraja}}}}}
], for which the purification process is typically employed as the initial step. In most research and industrial practice, vacuum distillation at high temperatures (150-200 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}C) is used to obtain refined-grade (99.5 wt %) glycerol 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Isahak2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{9}{2015}{{Isahak et\~al.}}{{Isahak, Ramli, Ismail, Jahim, and Yarmo}}}}}
], introducing large capital and operating costs. For this reason, we recently described a universal procedure, without high temperature distillation, to purify crude glycerol from a variety of biodiesel plants yielding essentially pure glycerol with a potentially lower cost. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2013 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{10}{2013}{{Xiao et\~al.}}{{Xiao, Xiao, and Varma}}}}}
]. Hydrocarbon fuels, including gasoline and diesel, provide high energy density and ease of transportation, playing an important role in nowadays global energy supply. With current cheap and readily available resource, conversion of glycerol to hydrocarbons (GTH) fuels would increase the economic value of glycerol and the feasibility of biomass as an energy source.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 In our prior work, bifunctional catalysts (Pt/H-ZSM-5 and Pd/H-ZSM-5) were selected, prepared, characterized, and tested for GTH conversion. It was found that under optimal conditions, about 90% glycerol conversion and 60% yield of aromatic hydrocarbons were achieved over Pd/H-ZSM-5 catalyst. A mechanism of sequential hydrodeoxgenation and aromatization was proposed for GTH conversion. Similar works, using various catalysts such as PdO/H-ZSM-5, Zn/H-ZSM-5 and Sn/H-ZSM-5 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Hoang2010 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{11}{2010}{{Hoang et\~al.}}{{Hoang, Zhu, Danuthai, Lobban, Resasco, and Mallinson}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Jang2014 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{12}{2014}{{Jang et\~al.}}{{Jang, Bae, Shin, Kim, Kim, and Suh}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2015b \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{13}{2015}{{Xiao et\~al.}}{{Xiao, Wang, and Xiao}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Tamiyakul2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{14}{2015}{{Tamiyakul et\~al.}}{{Tamiyakul, Ubolcharoen, Tungasmita, and Jongpatiwut}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Wang2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{15}{2016}{{Wang et\~al.}}{{Wang, Xiao, Gao, and Xiao}}}}}
] were reported. The GTH conversion is a complex process, involving more than 20 species, such as acetaldehyde, propenal (acrolein), hydroxyacetone, ethylene and C{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 6})}}{\fldrslt }}
}-C{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 10})}}{\fldrslt }}
} aromatic hydrocarbons, etc. The reaction pathways, including dehydration, dehydrogenation/hydrogenation and oligmerization, hence have been proposed 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Tamiyakul2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{14}{2015}{{Tamiyakul et\~al.}}{{Tamiyakul, Ubolcharoen, Tungasmita, and Jongpatiwut}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Wang2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{15}{2016}{{Wang et\~al.}}{{Wang, Xiao, Gao, and Xiao}}}}}
]. The kinetic study of GTH conversion, however, is limited. For such complex reaction networks, containing a large number of reacting components, lumped kinetic models are typically developed to represent the overall reaction pathways 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Nace1971 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{16}{1971}{{Nace et\~al.}}{{Nace, Voltz, and Weekman}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Jacob1976 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{17}{1976}{{Jacob et\~al.}}{{Jacob, Gross, Voltz, and Weekman}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Astarita1989 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{18}{1989}{{Astarita}}{{}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Keipert1995 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{19}{1995}{{Keipert et\~al.}}{{Keipert, Wolf, Schulz, and Baerns}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Olcese2013 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{20}{2013}{{Olcese et\~al.}}{{Olcese, Francois, Bettahar, Petitjean, and Dufour}}}}}
], which can be further used for reactor design and process scale-up. In the present work, based on our prior work 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{21}{2016}{{Xiao and Varma}}{{Xiao, and Varma}}}}}
], for the first time to our knowledge, differential kinetic experiments of GTH conversion were carried out, a lumped kinetic model was developed and reaction pathway/mechanism were discussed and compared with literature.\par
\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb300 \fi0 3  Experimental\par
\pard\plain\s4\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 3.1  Materials, Catalyst Preparation and Characterization\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 Palladium (II) nitrate hydrate (99.8% metals basis), from Alfa Aesar, was used as Pd metal precursor. ZSM-5 (Si/Al=40) of ammonium form was supplied by Zeolyst International. Pure glycerol of ACS grade and 37 wt% HCl were obtained from Mallinckrodt Chemicals. The chemical standards, including acetaldehyde, benzene, cumene, ethylene, hydroxyacetone (acetol), propanal, propenal (acrolein), propylene, toluene, m-xylene, o-xylene and p-xylene, were from Sigma Aldrich. Ultra high purity grade gases (H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, He, O{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, CH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, etc.) were purchased from Indiana Oxygen. \par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 The ammonium form of ZSM-5 (Si/Al=40) was converted to its H-form (H-ZSM-5) by calcining in air at 500 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} for 4 hours. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Chiang2010 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{22}{2010}{{Chiang and Bhan}}{{Chiang, and Bhan}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Iorio2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{23}{2015}{{Iorio et\~al.}}{{Iorio, Bates, Verma, Delgass, Ribeiro, Miller, and Gounder}}}}}
] The 5 wt%Pd/H-ZSM-5 catalyst was prepared by impregnation method.i Briefly, Pd precursor was dissolved in deionized water, and then added dropwise to the well stirred H-ZSM-5 slurry, with pH=5.6 and stirring continued for at least 8 hrs at room temperature. The slurry was then rinsed, dried in air at 100 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}. As noted above, the Pd loading was 5 wt%, while H-ZSM-5 was the H-form ZSM-5 with a Si/Al ratio of 40. In later sections, for this reason, metal loadings and details of Si/Al ratios in H-ZSM-5 are not noted explicitly when describing catalysts. Thus, Pd/H-ZSM-5 refers to 5 wt% Pd was loaded on H-form of ZSM-5 with a Si/Al ratio of 40.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 Since the same 5 wt% Pd/H-ZSM-5 catalyst, as in our prior publication 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{21}{2016}{{Xiao and Varma}}{{Xiao, and Varma}}}}}
], was used in the present work, results of catalyst characterization, including BET specific surface area, mean pore diameter, Pd metal dispersion (by H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}-O{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} titration 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Benson1965 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{24}{1965}{{Benson and Boudart}}{{Benson, and Boudart}}}}}
]), Pd particle size, AEC-ICP element analysis, NH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}-TPD, transmission electron microscopy (TEM), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and X-ray powder diffraction (XRD), can be found in that publication and its Supplementary Information. Briefly, the high BET surface area ({>}400 m{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}/g) and moderate mean pore diameter (about 0.5 nm) of Pd/H-ZSM-5 indicates good accessibility of reactants (e.g. glycerol, acrolein and olefins) over Pd/H-ZSM-5 catalysts. Both surface area and mean pore diameter data are consistent with those reported in the literature. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Serrano_Ruiz2011 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{25}{2011}{{Serrano-Ruiz and Dumesic}}{{Serrano-Ruiz, and Dumesic}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Olson1981 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{26}{1981}{{Olson et\~al.}}{{Olson, Kokotailo, Lawton, and Meier}}}}}
] The Pd particle sizes, provided by TEM (3.8 nm) and H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}-O{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} titration (3.2 nm) techniques, are consistent, corresponding to a Pd metal dispersion of 29.7%. The XRD patterns for Pd/H-ZSM-5 are similar to unsupported MFI (ZSM-5), as reported in the International Zeolite Association (IZA) structure database. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Baerlocher2007 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{27}{2007}{{Baerlocher et\~al.}}{{Baerlocher, McCusker, and Olson}}}}}
] No Pd peaks were identified, likely due to high metal dispersions (29.7%) and low metal loading content (5 wt%). 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Zhu2013 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{28}{2013}{{Zhu et\~al.}}{{Zhu, Wegener, Xie, Uche, Neurock, and Marks}}}}}
] For Pd/H-ZSM-5 catalysts, three 8-hour runs were conducted and no significant deactivation was found. As the AES-ICP element analysis shows, in a relatively small 4-7% metal leaching after three 8-hour runs, while for a single 8-hour runm metal leaching is less than 0.5%. This loss is unavoidable and is consistent with other works. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Antolini2006 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{29}{2006}{{Antolini et\~al.}}{{Antolini, Salgado, and Gonzalez}}}}}
] The NH{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}-TPD measurements showed that Bronsted acid peaks at 600-700 K 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Iorio2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{23}{2015}{{Iorio et\~al.}}{{Iorio, Bates, Verma, Delgass, Ribeiro, Miller, and Gounder}}}}}
] of Pd/H-ZSM-5. \par
\pard\plain\s4\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb180 \fi0 3.2  Kinetic Measurements and Product Analysis\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 The experiments were conducted in a continuous fixed-bed reactor system, as described in our prior work. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{21}{2016}{{Xiao and Varma}}{{Xiao, and Varma}}}}}
] The catalysts were packed in a stainless steel fixed-bed reactor (with an internal diameter = 10 mm). Prior to kinetics measurements, catalysts were activated at 400 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}C, 1 atm for 4 h under a gas mixture (H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} 25%, N{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} 75%) flow. Feedstocks were heated to their boiling points to ensure evaporation prior to being pumped by an IP 25 Isocratic pump from Dionex into the reactor. The product mixture was condensed in a dual-wall glass condenser, cooled down by propanediol circulation (to -20 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}C) controlled by a circulator (Thermo Haake C10). The liquid products were collected every 10 min and then analyzed, while the gaseous products were analyzed on-line every 5 min. The standard operating conditions were 400 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}C, 1 atm total pressure, 0.05 g catalyst, mixed gas of N{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} at flow rates 100 mL/min and 50 mL/min respectively, and pure glycerol feed rate 0.3 mL/h (liquid, at room temperature). Based on standard operating procedure, to obtain kinetic data under various conditions, parameters, including temperature, reactant feed flow, catalyst packing amount were varied. All experiments were conducted under excess hydrogen flow (feed molar ratio of H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} to glycerol {>} 10). A blank test with packing inert material (amorphous SiO{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}), instead of Pd/H-ZSM-5, was conducted under the standard reaction conditions. It was found that glycerol conversion was {<} 1%. All experiments have mass balances of 90 {{\u177*}} 3%, which was consistent with literature 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Jongerius2013 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{30}{2013}{{Jongerius et\~al.}}{{Jongerius, Bruijnincx, and Weckhuysen}}}}}
]. Possible factors affecting mass balance include liquid hold-up in the reactor, condenser and tubing.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 Products from GTH process split into three phases: incondensable gaseous, upper organic liquid and lower aqueous liquid, which were analyzed by three devices respectively. The gases were analyzed by a micro-GC (Agilent 3000A) equipped with a thermal conductivity detector, a Mol-Sieve 5A column and a Plot U column. The upper organic liquid products were analyzed by an Agilent GC 6890 equipped with flame ionization detector (FID) and a DB-WAX (30 m {{\u215*}} 0.32 mm) column, while the lower aqueous liquid samples were analyzed by an Agilent GC 5890 equipped with a FID and Select Biodiesel for glycerides ultiMetal Column (15m {{\u215*}} 0.32 mm, ID=0.10 nm) and a retention gap. Calibration was performed for all the components. \par
\pard\plain\s3\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb300 \fi0 4  Results and discussion\par
\pard\plain\s4\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi0 4.1  Proposed reaction pathways\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 It was described in our prior work 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{21}{2016}{{Xiao and Varma}}{{Xiao, and Varma}}}}}
], as shown in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMT_products \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Table\~ {}1}}}, that oxygenates (acrolein, acetol, etc.) were primary products if unsupported H-ZSM-5, Pd/SiO{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} (amorphous) and Pt/SiO{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} (amorphous) were used as catalysts, while the use of H-ZSM-5 preferred to produce aromatic hydrocarbon fuels. The addition of noble metal (Pd or Pt) to H-ZSM-5, as compared to unsupported H-ZSM-5, enhanced yields towards aromatics and decreased the selectivity to oxygenates. It thus indicates that oxygenates are possible primary intermediates for GTH conversion. Similar intermediates were also reported elsewhere 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Tamiyakul2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{14}{2015}{{Tamiyakul et\~al.}}{{Tamiyakul, Ubolcharoen, Tungasmita, and Jongpatiwut}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Wang2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{15}{2016}{{Wang et\~al.}}{{Wang, Xiao, Gao, and Xiao}}}}}
]. The reaction pathways, as shown in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_network \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Figure\~ {}1}}}, therefore are proposed for GTH conversion, in which two possible pathways are considered: glycerol, oxygenates, gases and aromatics (steps R1, R2, followed by R4); glycerol, oxygenates and aromatics (step R1 followed by R3)\par
