\pard\plain\s30\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 {Figure {\*\bkmkstart BMF_network}1{\*\bkmkend BMF_network}: Glycerol to Hydrocarbon (GTH) Reaction Network}{\field{\*\fldinst TC "1 Glycerol to Hydrocarbon (GTH) Reaction Network" \\f f}{\fldrslt }}\par
}\pard\plain\s4\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb360 \fi0 4.2  Absence of Mass and Heat Transfer Effects\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 As shown in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_transfer \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Figure\~ {}2}}}, using the well known procedure, the absence of both internal and external mass transfer limitations were confirmed before conducting kinetic measurements. By only varying packed catalyst particle diameter ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}) in the range of 100 micron < {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} < 1000 micron, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_transfer \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Figure\~ {}2}}}(a) was obtained. It shows that when {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} {<} 150 micron (corresponding to 100 mesh), internal mass transfer effect was negligible. {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_transfer \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Figure\~ {}2}}}(b) was obtained by varying feed flow rates, while keeping other operating, including contact time (W/F), conditions unchanged. It indicates that when the feed flow rate {>} 0.4 mL/min, no significant external mass transfer effect was found. Thus, ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i p}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}) < 150 micron and feed flow rate larger than 0.4 mL/min were used in kinetic measurements. Further, the criteria, as described by Weisz and Prater 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Weisz1954 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{31}{1954}{{Weisz and Prater}}{{Weisz, and Prater}}}}}
] and Mears 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Mears1971 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{32}{1971}{{Mears}}{{}}}}}
], were both satisfied as well, confirming the absence of mass and heat transfer effects in all kinetic measurement experiments.\par
