}\pard\plain\s4\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb360 \fi0 4.3  Kinetic model development\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 In the reaction pathways shown in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_network \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Figure\~ {}1}}}, each step (R1-R4) contains more than one elementary reaction. Step R1, converting glycerol to arolein, involves two sequential dehydration reactions 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Gu2012 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{33}{2012}{{Gu et\~al.}}{{Gu, Cui, Yu, Li, and Cui}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Zhang2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{34}{2015}{{Zhang et\~al.}}{{Zhang, Hu, Huang, Zhang, Song, Wang, Shi, Ma, Zhuang, Shen, Zhang, Xu, and Tang}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Galadima2016b \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{35}{2016}{{Galadima and Muraza}}{{Galadima, and Muraza}}}}}
], while more complex reactions (e.g. hydrogenation, dehydrogenation, deoxygenation, etc.) exist when other oxygenates are produced 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Park2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{36}{2015}{{Park et\~al.}}{{Park, Yun, Kim, Lee, Baek, and Yi}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Tamiyakul2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{14}{2015}{{Tamiyakul et\~al.}}{{Tamiyakul, Ubolcharoen, Tungasmita, and Jongpatiwut}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Wang2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{15}{2016}{{Wang et\~al.}}{{Wang, Xiao, Gao, and Xiao}}}}}
]. Step R2 covers deoxygenation, dehydrogenation and C-C bond cleavage reactions, while step R4 includes oligmerization, cyclization and dehydrogenation. Step 3 refers to pathways where C{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 1})}}{\fldrslt }}
}-C{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
} gases do not exist. Thus the overall GTH conversion covers a complex network, which can be reduced to a few equivalent lumped reactions: steps R1-R4. As lumped pseudo-components, oxygenates represent a mixture of acetaldehyde, propanal, acrolein and hydroxyacetone (acetol). Gases represent a mixture of methane, ethylene and propylene, while aromatics refer to C{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 6})}}{\fldrslt }}
}-C{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 12})}}{\fldrslt }}
} aromatic hydrocarbons.\par
