}\pard\plain\s4\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb180 \fi0 4.4  Glycerol to Oxygenates (Step R1)\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 Since excess H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} (molar ratio of H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} to glycerol is {{\f5\u8764*}}10:1) is used in all cases, H{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} pressure effect was considered as a constant contribution to the reaction rates. Kinetics measurements were carried out at various temperatures in the range of 350-450 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}C. Although only results at 400 {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \\s\\up5({\fs16 {\u8728*}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} C, as the standard temperature, are shown below, which gives optimum aromatic yields 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{21}{2016}{{Xiao and Varma}}{{Xiao, and Varma}}}}}
], fitting results for other temperatures presented essentially the same conclusions. All experiments were repeated at least twice, while temperature effects are discussed in later section.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 When glycerol was fed, under differential operating conditions (glycerol conversion typically {<} 5-10%), all lumped components (oxygenates, gases and aromatics) were detected. By varying glycerol feeding flow rates, the consuming rates of glycerol, with respect to average bed reactant partial pressures, were measured, as shown and fitted in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_glycerol \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Figure\~ {}3}}}.\par
