\pard\plain\s30\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 {Figure {\*\bkmkstart BMF_glycerol}3{\*\bkmkend BMF_glycerol}: Fit of Glycerol Initial Reaction Rates}{\field{\*\fldinst TC "3 Fit of Glycerol Initial Reaction Rates" \\f f}{\fldrslt }}\par
}\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi40 As described by Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq4 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{4}}}), the consuming rates of glycerol are only dependent on glycerol partial pressure. Since a linear fit works well for glycerol initial reaction rates, step R1 appears to be a first-order reaction, which is consistent with literature 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Akizuki2012 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{37}{2012}{{Akizuki and Oshima}}{{Akizuki, and Oshima}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Talebian_Kiakalaieh2014 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{38}{2014}{{Talebian-Kiakalaieh et\~al.}}{{Talebian-Kiakalaieh, Amin, and Hezaveh}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Park2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{36}{2015}{{Park et\~al.}}{{Park, Yun, Kim, Lee, Baek, and Yi}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Talebian_Kiakalaieh2015 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{39}{2015}{{Talebian-Kiakalaieh and Amin}}{{Talebian-Kiakalaieh, and Amin}}}}}
] Thus, Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq4 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{4}}}) can be re-written as Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq11 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{10}}}).\par
{\pard\plain\s12\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 \tab
{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \u8722? \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i L}{\i Y}}),{\i d}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i W}}))={\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 1})={\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 1}){\i P}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i L}{\i Y}})}}{\fldrslt }}
\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq11}10{\*\bkmkend BMEq11})}\par
}\pard\plain\s4\ql\sb240\sa120\keepn\f0\b\fs32\sl240\slmult1 \sb180 \fi0 4.5  Oxygenates as Reactant (Steps R2 and R3)\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb120 \fi0 In the above section, the rate constant and reaction order of step R1 were obtained by feeding glycerol in a differential reactor, controlling glycerol conversions were under 5-10 %. To study the rate constants and reaction orders of steps R2-R4, still under differential operating conditions, oxygenates were used as feed. The oxygenates, based on the compositions reported in our prior work 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Xiao2016 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{21}{2016}{{Xiao and Varma}}{{Xiao, and Varma}}}}}
], were represented by a mixture of acetaldehyde, propanal, acrolein and acetol. Because acrolein was found to be the primary component (60-93%), pure acrolein was also tested as a representative component. As shown in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_oxygenates \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Figure\~ {}4}}}, both oxygenates and pure acrolein curves show good secondary-order fits, while generating rates of gases and aromatics, corresponding to {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}-{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}+{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, appears to be secondary orders as well.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 The conversion of acrolein to gases (olefins) likely includes hydrogenation of arolein to propanol, followed by propanol dehydration and other C-C cleavage reactions. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Marinelli1995 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{40}{1995}{{Marinelli and Ponec}}{{Marinelli, and Ponec}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Mohr2000 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{41}{2000}{{Mohr et\~al.}}{{Mohr, Hofmeister, Lucas, and Claus}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Volckmar2009 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{42}{2009}{{Volckmar et\~al.}}{{Volckmar, Bron, Bentrup, Martin, and Claus}}}}}
]. Both first and second order were reported for acrolein hydrogenation. 
[{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Jesus1999 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{43}{1999}{{de\~Jesus and Zaera}}{{de\~Jesus, and Zaera}}}}}, {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BIB_Wei2013 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{{44}{2013}{{Wei et\~al.}}{{Wei, Gomez, Liu, Guo, Wu, Lobo-Lapidus, Marshall, Miller, and Meyer}}}}}
] Since reaction rates starting with representative oxygenates and pure acrolein are close, as shown in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_oxygenates \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Figure\~ {}4}}}, only pure arolein was used for further kinetic measurements, discussion and analysis.\par
