\pard\plain\s30\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\sl240\slmult1 \fi0 {Figure {\*\bkmkstart BMF_oxygenates}4{\*\bkmkend BMF_oxygenates}: Fit of Oxygenate Initial Reaction Rates}{\field{\*\fldinst TC "4 Fit of Oxygenate Initial Reaction Rates" \\f f}{\fldrslt }}\par
}\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb240 \fi40 Both gases and aromatics were detected when oxygenates conversions were lower than 10%. Since no glycerol was fed ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 1})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=0), the consuming rate of oxygenates is dependent only on {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, as described by Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq5 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{5}}}). {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_oxygenates \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Figure\~ {}4}}} shows that the overall reaction rates of {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
} + {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
} follow a secondary order. It is assumed here that reaction orders of steps R2 and R3 are the same, thus {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}={{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=2. Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq5 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{5}}} -\~{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq7 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{7}}}) therefore are re-written as Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq12 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{11}}} -\~{\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq15 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{13}}}).\par
{\pard\plain\s12\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 \tab
{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ \u8722? \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}}),{\i d}{\i W})={\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})+{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})={\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})+{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})}}{\fldrslt }}
\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq12}11{\*\bkmkend BMEq12})}\par
}{\pard\plain\s12\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 \tab
{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ  \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i A}{\i S}}),{\i d}{\i W})={\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})\u8722?{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})={\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})\u8722?{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 {\i n}\\s\\do4({\fs13 4}),{\i G}{\i A}{\i S}})}}{\fldrslt }}
\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq14}12{\*\bkmkend BMEq14})}\par
}{\pard\plain\s12\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 \tab
{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ  \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i A}{\i R}{\i O}}),{\i d}{\i W})={\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})+{\i r}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})={\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})+{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 {\i n}\\s\\do4({\fs13 4}),{\i G}{\i A}{\i S}})}}{\fldrslt }}
\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq15}13{\*\bkmkend BMEq15})}\par
}\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 To determine reaction order of step R4 ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
}) and all rate constants ({{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}-{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})}}{\fldrslt }}
}), Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq16 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{14}}}) is obtained by Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq15 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{13}}}) / Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq14 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{12}}}):\par
{\pard\plain\s12\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 \tab
{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ  \\F({\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i A}{\i R}{\i O}}),{\i d}{\i F}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i A}{\i S}}))= \\F({\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})+{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 {\i n}\\s\\do4({\fs13 4}),{\i G}{\i A}{\i S}}),{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})\u8722?{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4}){\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 {\i n}\\s\\do4({\fs13 4}),{\i G}{\i A}{\i S}}))={\u945*}}}{\fldrslt }}
\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq16}14{\*\bkmkend BMEq16})}\par
}\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 With respect to partial pressure of gases, the aromatics partial pressure is plotted in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_234 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Figure\~ {}5}}}(a). Since a linear correlation fits well, as shown in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_234 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Figure\~ {}5}}}(a), for {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i P}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i A}{\i R}{\i O}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} versus {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i P}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i A}{\i S}})}}{\fldrslt }}
}, Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq16 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{14}}}) equals a constant, which is assigned as {{\u945*}}.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 Thus, the following Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq17 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{15}}}) also equals a constant, which is assigned as {{\u946*}}.\par
{\pard\plain\s12\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 \tab
{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ  \\F({\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 {\i n}\\s\\do4({\fs13 4}),{\i G}{\i A}{\i S}}),{\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}}))= \\F({\u945*}{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})\u8722?{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3}),{\i k}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4})({\u945*}+1))={\u946*}}}{\fldrslt }}
\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq17}15{\*\bkmkend BMEq17})}\par
}\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 Thus, Eq. ({\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMEq18 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{16}}}) is obtained by the use of logarithm forms.\par
{\pard\plain\s12\ql\sb120\sa120\keep\widctlpar\f0\tqc\tx3450\tqr\tx6900\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi0 \tab
{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i l}{\i o}{\i g}{\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})={\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 4}){\i l}{\i o}{\i g}{\i P}\\s\\do5({\fs16 {\i G}{\i A}{\i S}})\u8722?{\i l}{\i o}{\i g}{\u946*}}}{\fldrslt }}
\tab{\b0 ({\*\bkmkstart BMEq18}16{\*\bkmkend BMEq18})}\par
}\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 The correlation of {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 {\i n}\\s\\do4({\fs13 4}),{\i G}{\i A}{\i S}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i P}\\s\\up({\fs16 2,{\i O}{\i X}{\i Y}})}}{\fldrslt }}
} are plotted in {\field{\*\fldinst{\lang1024 REF BMF_234 \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt{Figure\~ {}5}}} (b), where the fitted slope appears to be 0.968. Thus step 4 is found to be a first-order reaction.\par
\pard\plain\s0\qj\widctlpar\f0\fs20\sl240\slmult1 \sb60 \fi40 More tests for reaction orders of steps R2 and R3 were carried out, such as the following data sets: {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=1.0, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=1.0; {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=1.0, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=2.0; {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=2.0, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=1.0;{{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=2.0, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=3.0; and {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=3.0, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=2.0, etc. Data fit under these sets always provide worse fits. Thus it is confirmed that the assumption of {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 2})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=2.0, {{\field{\*\fldinst{ EQ {\i n}\\s\\do5({\fs16 3})}}{\fldrslt }}
}=2.0 is reasonable.\par
